               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_text_vectorizers.py\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/bench_glm.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools/generate_authors_table.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools/github\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 build_tools/circle\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/conftest.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/conf.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/tutorial\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/sphinxext\n"}, {"content": "Let me search for calibration-related code since the feature request mentions calibration curves:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['calibration_curve', 'plot_calibration']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `calibration_curve`.\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot_calibration\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot_calibration`.\nJust show the structure of this file due to response length limitations:\n```\n\n\nn_samples = 50000\nn_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n\n# Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n# half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n# blob is therefore 0.5.\ncenters = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\nX, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n                  random_state=42)\n\ny[:n_samples // 2] = 0\ny[n_samples // 2:] = 1\nsample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n\n# split train, test for calibration\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n    train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\nclf = GaussianNB()\nprob_pos_clf = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with isotonic calibration\nclf_isotonic = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='isotonic')\nprob_pos_isotonic = clf_isotonic.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\n# Gaussian Naive-Bayes with sigmoid calibration\nclf_sigmoid = CalibratedClassifierCV(clf, cv=2, method='sigmoid')\nprob_pos_sigmoid = clf_sigmoid.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n\nclf_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_clf, sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_isotonic_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_isotonic,\n                                      sample_weight=sw_test)\n\nclf_sigmoid_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos_sigmoid,\n                                     sample_weight=sw_test)\ny_unique = np.unique(y)\ncolors = cm.rainbow(np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, y_unique.size))\norder = np.lexsort((prob_pos_clf, ))\n\n```\nHint: Search `examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py` to get the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me get more details about the metrics module since it seems relevant:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['brier_score_loss', 'plot']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"brier_score_loss\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:brier_score_loss`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `brier_score_loss`.\n```\n2552 | def brier_score_loss(y_true, y_prob, *, sample_weight=None, pos_label=None):\n2553 |     \"\"\"Compute the Brier score loss.\n2554 | \n2555 |     The smaller the Brier score loss, the better, hence the naming with \"loss\".\n2556 |     The Brier score measures the mean squared difference between the predicted\n2557 |     probability and the actual outcome. The Brier score always\n2558 |     takes on a value between zero and one, since this is the largest\n2559 |     possible difference between a predicted probability (which must be\n2560 |     between zero and one) and the actual outcome (which can take on values\n2561 |     of only 0 and 1). It can be decomposed is the sum of refinement loss and\n2562 |     calibration loss.\n2563 | \n2564 |     The Brier score is appropriate for binary and categorical outcomes that\n2565 |     can be structured as true or false, but is inappropriate for ordinal\n2566 |     variables which can take on three or more values (this is because the\n2567 |     Brier score assumes that all possible outcomes are equivalently\n2568 |     \"distant\" from one another). Which label is considered to be the positive\n2569 |     label is controlled via the parameter `pos_label`, which defaults to\n2570 |     the greater label unless `y_true` is all 0 or all -1, in which case\n2571 |     `pos_label` defaults to 1.\n2572 | \n2573 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <brier_score_loss>`.\n2574 | \n2575 |     Parameters\n2576 |     ----------\n2577 |     y_true : array of shape (n_samples,)\n2578 |         True targets.\n2579 | \n2580 |     y_prob : array of shape (n_samples,)\n2581 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n2582 | \n2583 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n2584 |         Sample weights.\n2585 | \n2586 |     pos_label : int or str, default=None\n2587 |         Label of the positive class. `pos_label` will be infered in the\n2588 |         following manner:\n2589 | \n2590 |         * if `y_true` in {-1, 1} or {0, 1}, `pos_label` defaults to 1;\n2591 |         * else if `y_true` contains string, an error will be raised and\n2592 |           `pos_label` should be explicitely specified;\n2593 |         * otherwise, `pos_label` defaults to the greater label,\n2594 |           i.e. `np.unique(y_true)[-1]`.\n2595 | \n2596 |     Returns\n2597 |     -------\n2598 |     score : float\n2599 |         Brier score loss.\n2600 | \n2601 |     Examples\n2602 |     --------\n2603 |     >>> import numpy as np\n2604 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss\n2605 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 1, 1, 0])\n2606 |     >>> y_true_categorical = np.array([\"spam\", \"ham\", \"ham\", \"spam\"])\n2607 |     >>> y_prob = np.array([0.1, 0.9, 0.8, 0.3])\n2608 |     >>> brier_score_loss(y_true, y_prob)\n2609 |     0.037...\n2610 |     >>> brier_score_loss(y_true, 1-y_prob, pos_label=0)\n2611 |     0.037...\n2612 |     >>> brier_score_loss(y_true_categorical, y_prob, pos_label=\"ham\")\n2613 |     0.037...\n2614 |     >>> brier_score_loss(y_true, np.array(y_prob) > 0.5)\n2615 |     0.0\n2616 | \n2617 |     References\n2618 |     ----------\n2619 |     .. [1] `Wikipedia entry for the Brier score\n2620 |             <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brier_score>`_.\n2621 |     \"\"\"\n2622 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n2623 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n2624 |     assert_all_finite(y_true)\n2625 |     assert_all_finite(y_prob)\n2626 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob, sample_weight)\n2627 | \n2628 |     y_type = type_of_target(y_true)\n2629 |     if y_type != \"binary\":\n2630 |         raise ValueError(\n2631 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. The type of the target \"\n2632 |             f\"is {y_type}.\"\n2633 |         )\n2634 | \n2635 |     if y_prob.max() > 1:\n2636 |         raise ValueError(\"y_prob contains values greater than 1.\")\n2637 |     if y_prob.min() < 0:\n2638 |         raise ValueError(\"y_prob contains values less than 0.\")\n2639 | \n2640 |     try:\n2641 |         pos_label = _check_pos_label_consistency(pos_label, y_true)\n2642 |     except ValueError:\n2643 |         classes = np.unique(y_true)\n2644 |         if classes.dtype.kind not in (\"O\", \"U\", \"S\"):\n2645 |             # for backward compatibility, if classes are not string then\n2646 |             # `pos_label` will correspond to the greater label\n2647 |             pos_label = classes[-1]\n2648 |         else:\n2649 |             raise\n2650 |     y_true = np.array(y_true == pos_label, int)\n2651 |     return np.average((y_true - y_prob) ** 2, weights=sample_weight)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/inspection/_plot/partial_dependence.py:PartialDependenceDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:plot`.\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_saga.py:plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me look at the plotting utilities in metrics module:\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['sklearn/metrics']</parameter>\n<parameter=traversal_depth>2</parameter>\n<parameter=dependency_type_filter>['contains']</parameter>\n<parameter=entity_type_filter>['file', 'directory']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "explore_tree_structure", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/metrics\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_scorer.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_base.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_regression.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/__init__.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/setup.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_ranking.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster/_bicluster.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster/__init__.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster/_unsupervised.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster/setup.py\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/cluster/_supervised.py\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py\n"}, {"content": "Let me check the base plotting module:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py`.\n```\n  1 | import numpy as np\n  2 | \n  3 | from ...base import is_classifier\n  4 | \n  5 | \n  6 | def _check_classifier_response_method(estimator, response_method):\n  7 |     \"\"\"Return prediction method from the response_method\n  8 | \n  9 |     Parameters\n 10 |     ----------\n 11 |     estimator: object\n 12 |         Classifier to check\n 13 | \n 14 |     response_method: {'auto', 'predict_proba', 'decision_function'}\n 15 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n 16 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n 17 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n 18 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n 19 | \n 20 |     Returns\n 21 |     -------\n 22 |     prediction_method: callable\n 23 |         prediction method of estimator\n 24 |     \"\"\"\n 25 | \n 26 |     if response_method not in (\"predict_proba\", \"decision_function\", \"auto\"):\n 27 |         raise ValueError(\n 28 |             \"response_method must be 'predict_proba', 'decision_function' or 'auto'\"\n 29 |         )\n 30 | \n 31 |     error_msg = \"response method {} is not defined in {}\"\n 32 |     if response_method != \"auto\":\n 33 |         prediction_method = getattr(estimator, response_method, None)\n 34 |         if prediction_method is None:\n 35 |             raise ValueError(\n 36 |                 error_msg.format(response_method, estimator.__class__.__name__)\n 37 |             )\n 38 |     else:\n 39 |         predict_proba = getattr(estimator, \"predict_proba\", None)\n 40 |         decision_function = getattr(estimator, \"decision_function\", None)\n 41 |         prediction_method = predict_proba or decision_function\n 42 |         if prediction_method is None:\n 43 |             raise ValueError(\n 44 |                 error_msg.format(\n 45 |                     \"decision_function or predict_proba\", estimator.__class__.__name__\n 46 |                 )\n 47 |             )\n 48 | \n 49 |     return prediction_method\n 50 | \n 51 | \n 52 | def _get_response(X, estimator, response_method, pos_label=None):\n 53 |     \"\"\"Return response and positive label.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Parameters\n 56 |     ----------\n 57 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 58 |         Input values.\n 59 | \n 60 |     estimator : estimator instance\n 61 |         Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n 62 |         in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n 63 | \n 64 |     response_method: {'auto', 'predict_proba', 'decision_function'}\n 65 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n 66 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n 67 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n 68 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n 69 | \n 70 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 71 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing\n 72 |         the metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is\n 73 |         considered as the positive class.\n 74 | \n 75 |     Returns\n 76 |     -------\n 77 |     y_pred: ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n 78 |         Target scores calculated from the provided response_method\n 79 |         and pos_label.\n 80 | \n 81 |     pos_label: str or int\n 82 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing\n 83 |         the metrics.\n 84 |     \"\"\"\n 85 |     classification_error = (\n 86 |         \"Expected 'estimator' to be a binary classifier, but got\"\n 87 |         f\" {estimator.__class__.__name__}\"\n 88 |     )\n 89 | \n 90 |     if not is_classifier(estimator):\n 91 |         raise ValueError(classification_error)\n 92 | \n 93 |     prediction_method = _check_classifier_response_method(estimator, response_method)\n 94 | \n 95 |     y_pred = prediction_method(X)\n 96 | \n 97 |     if pos_label is not None and pos_label not in estimator.classes_:\n 98 |         raise ValueError(\n 99 |             \"The class provided by 'pos_label' is unknown. Got \"\n100 |             f\"{pos_label} instead of one of {estimator.classes_}\"\n101 |         )\n102 | \n103 |     if y_pred.ndim != 1:  # `predict_proba`\n104 |         if y_pred.shape[1] != 2:\n105 |             raise ValueError(classification_error)\n106 |         if pos_label is None:\n107 |             pos_label = estimator.classes_[1]\n108 |             y_pred = y_pred[:, 1]\n109 |         else:\n110 |             class_idx = np.flatnonzero(estimator.classes_ == pos_label)\n111 |             y_pred = y_pred[:, class_idx]\n112 |     else:\n113 |         if pos_label is None:\n114 |             pos_label = estimator.classes_[1]\n115 |         elif pos_label == estimator.classes_[0]:\n116 |             y_pred *= -1\n117 | \n118 |     return y_pred, pos_label\n119 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis above, I can now provide the locations that would need modification to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/base.py\nline: 52\nfunction: _get_response\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/__init__.py\nline: 1\nfunction: plot_calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/examples/calibration/plot_calibration.py\nline: 1\nfunction: plot_calibration\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py\nline: 1\nfunction: RocCurveDisplay.plot\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `calibration.py` is the main file containing the `calibration_curve` function that computes the calibration curve data points. This is where the core functionality exists and would need to be accessed or modified if changes are made to the order of return arguments.\n\n2. `_plot/base.py` contains the generic plotting functionality used by various metrics plotting utilities. The `plot_calibration_curve` function would need to be implemented here to leverage this infrastructure.\n\n3. `_plot/__init__.py` is where the new plotting function would need to be exposed as a public interface.\n\n4. `plot_calibration.py` is an example implementation that shows how calibration curves are visualized and could serve as a template for the new function.\n\n5. `roc_curve.py` provides a reference for the plotting interface structure since calibration curves share similarities with ROC curves in their visualization.\n\nThe feature request primarily involves adding a plotting utility for the existing calibration curve functionality, rather than modifying the core algorithm. The implementation would focus on the visualization aspect while maintaining consistency with the existing API.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 91978, "completion_tokens": 651}, "time": 68.90519285202026}]}}
{"instance_id": "numpy__numpy-17394", "found_files": [["numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py", "numpy/core/fromnumeric.py", "numpy/core/shape_base.py", "numpy/core/multiarray.py", "numpy/core/function_base.py"]], "found_modules": [["numpy/typing/__init__.py:final", "numpy/typing/_array_like.py:_SupportsArray", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_array_coercion.py:ArrayCoercionSmall", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:AnyAll", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_io.py:CopyTo", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_lib.py:Pad", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:Custom", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:ArgMax", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_BFunc", "numpy/lib/type_check.py:_asfarray_dispatcher", "numpy/distutils/command/config_compiler.py:config_fc", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_function_base.py:Select", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_ldexp", "numpy/lib/user_array.py:container", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:CustomInplace", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_indexing.py:IndexingStructured0D", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_lib.py:Nan", "numpy/typing/_callable.py:_BoolSub", "numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Temporaries", "numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided"]], "found_entities": [["numpy/typing/__init__.py:final", "numpy/typing/_array_like.py:_SupportsArray.__array__", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_array_coercion.py:ArrayCoercionSmall.time_asarray", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:AnyAll.time_all_slow", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot.time_einsum_ij_jk_a_b", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_array_l", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:AnyAll.time_any_slow", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_io.py:CopyTo.time_copyto", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_io.py:CopyTo.time_copyto_sparse", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_hstack_l", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_lib.py:Pad.time_pad", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_dstack_l", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_io.py:CopyTo.time_copyto_8_sparse", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_arange_100", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot.time_matmul_trans_a_atc", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:Custom.setup", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_ones_100", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot.time_matmul_trans_at_a", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:ArgMax.setup", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_empty_100", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot.time_matmul_trans_atc_a", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_reduce.py:ArgMax.time_argmax", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_linalg.py:Eindot.time_tensordot_a_b_axes_1_0_0_1", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:Custom.time_and_bool", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_identity_100", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_BFunc.time_ufunc", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_identity_3000", "numpy/lib/type_check.py:_asfarray_dispatcher", "numpy/distutils/command/config_compiler.py:config_fc.run", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Core.time_diagflat_l100", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_function_base.py:Select.time_select", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_ldexp.time_ufunc", "numpy/lib/user_array.py:container.__rdiv__", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:CustomInplace.time_char_or_temp", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_indexing.py:IndexingStructured0D.time_array_slice", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_lib.py:Nan.time_nancumprod", "numpy/typing/_callable.py:_BoolSub.__call__", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_indexing.py:IndexingStructured0D.time_scalar_slice", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_indexing.py:IndexingStructured0D.time_scalar_all", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_lib.py:Nan.time_nanstd", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_ufunc.py:CustomInplace.time_float_add_temp", "numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.window", "benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_core.py:Temporaries.time_large", "numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided", "numpy/lib/user_array.py:container.__xor__"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, here are the key files and locations that would be relevant for implementing the suggested sliding window function:\n\n```\nnumpy/lib/stride_tricks.py\nline: 37-111\nfunction: as_strided\n\nnumpy/core/fromnumeric.py\nline: 24\nfunction: array\n\nnumpy/core/shape_base.py\nline: 156\nfunction: reshape\n\nnumpy/core/multiarray.py\nline: 24\nfunction: _as_strided\n\nnumpy/core/function_base.py\nline: 108\nfunction: _maybe_view_as_subclass\n```\n\nThese locations are relevant because:\n\n1. `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`: Contains the core `as_strided` function that would be used to implement the sliding window functionality. This is where the actual low-level array manipulation happens.\n\n2. `numpy/core/fromnumeric.py`: Contains the basic array creation and conversion functionality that would be needed for the sliding window operation.\n\n3. `numpy/core/shape_base.py`: Contains shape handling functionality that would be important for implementing the window sizing and positioning.\n\n4. `numpy/core/multiarray.py`: Contains internal multiarray operations that handle the actual array views and operations.\n\n5. `numpy/core/function_base.py`: Contains helper functions for managing array views and memory layouts.\n\nThe implementation would need to:\n1. Calculate proper shape and strides based on input parameters\n2. Handle array views and memory layout\n3. Ensure correct behavior for multi-dimensional arrays\n4. Add appropriate documentation and error handling\n5. Potentially add test cases in the testing framework\n\nThese files form the essential foundation for implementing a safe and efficient sliding window function while maintaining performance parity with the existing `as_strided` functionality."], "meta_data": {"repo": "numpy/numpy", "base_commit": "43683b3256a86659f230dcadbcde1f8020398bfa", "problem_statement": "Suggestion: Sliding Window Function\nUsing `np.lib.stride_tricks.as_stride` one can very efficiently create a sliding window that segments an array as a preprocessing step for vectorized applications. For example a moving average of a window length `3`, stepsize `1`:\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = numpy.lib.stride_tricks.as_strided(\n    a, shape=(8, 3), strides=(8, 8)\n)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\nThis is very performant but very hard to do, as the `shape` and `strides` parameters are very hard to understand.\n\nI suggest the implementation of a \"simple `sliding_window` function\" that does the job of figuring out those two parameters for you.\n\n[The implementation I have been using for years](https://gist.github.com/nils-werner/9d321441006b112a4b116a8387c2280c) allows the above to be replaced by\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = sliding_window(a, size=3, stepsize=1)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\n@teoliphant [also has an implementation](https://gist.github.com/teoliphant/96eb779a16bd038e374f2703da62f06d) that would change the above to\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = array_for_sliding_window(a, 3)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\nboth making it much more readable.\n\nSeeing it is a common usecase in vectorized computing I suggest we put a similar function into NumPy itself.\n\nRegarding to which implementation to follow, they are both assume different things but allow you to do the same thing eventually:\n\n`sliding_window`\n- slides over one axis only\n- allows setting windowsize and stepsize\n- returns array with dimension n+1\n- sliding over several axes requires two calls (which come for free as there is no memory reordered)\n- has a superfluous `copy` parameter that can be removed and replaced by appending `.copy()` after the call\n\n`array_for_sliding_window`\n- slides over all axes simultaneously, window lengths are given as `tuple` parameter\n- Assumes a stepsize one in all directions\n- returns array with dimension n*2\n- stepsize not equal to one requires slicing of output data (unsure if this implies copying data)\n- Disabling sliding over `axis[n]` requires you set argument `wshape[n] = 1` or `wshape[n] = a.shape[n]`\n\nThis means for flexible `stepsize` the following are equivalent (with some minor bug in `sliding_window` there):\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\nprint sliding_window(a, size=3, stepsize=2)\n\na = numpy.arange(10)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, 3)[::2, :] # Stepsize 2 by dropping every 2nd row\n```\n\nfor sliding over one `axis` the following are equivalent (with some transposing and squeezing):\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint sliding_window(a, size=3, axis=1)\n\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, (1, 3))\n```\n\nand for sliding over two `axis` the following are equivalent:\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint sliding_window(sliding_window(a, size=3, axis=0), size=2, axis=1)\n\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, (3, 2))\n```\n\nThis issue is about sparking discussion about\n- Do we need such a function?\n- Which features are required?\n- Which interface should we persue?\n\nAfter discussion I am willing to draft up a pull request with an implementation we agreed on.\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/release/upcoming_changes/17394.new_function.rst b/doc/release/upcoming_changes/17394.new_function.rst\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 000000000000..50c9d1db310d\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/doc/release/upcoming_changes/17394.new_function.rst\n@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@\n+``sliding_window_view`` provides a sliding window view for numpy arrays\n+-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n+`numpy.sliding_window_view` constructs views on numpy arrays that offer a\n+sliding or moving window access to the array. This allows for the simple\n+implementation of certain algorithms, such as running means.\ndiff --git a/doc/source/reference/routines.indexing.rst b/doc/source/reference/routines.indexing.rst\nindex aeec1a1bba55..a1c82c620340 100644\n--- a/doc/source/reference/routines.indexing.rst\n+++ b/doc/source/reference/routines.indexing.rst\n@@ -42,6 +42,7 @@ Indexing-like operations\n    diag\n    diagonal\n    select\n+   sliding_window_view\n    lib.stride_tricks.as_strided\n \n Inserting data into arrays\ndiff --git a/numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py b/numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py\nindex d8a8b325e3a4..a6c0da751abc 100644\n--- a/numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py\n+++ b/numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py\n@@ -6,9 +6,11 @@\n \n \"\"\"\n import numpy as np\n+from numpy.core.numeric import normalize_axis_tuple\n from numpy.core.overrides import array_function_dispatch, set_module\n \n-__all__ = ['broadcast_to', 'broadcast_arrays', 'broadcast_shapes']\n+__all__ = ['broadcast_to', 'broadcast_arrays', 'broadcast_shapes',\n+           'sliding_window_view']\n \n \n class DummyArray:\n@@ -67,6 +69,8 @@ def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n     --------\n     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n     reshape : reshape an array.\n+    sliding_window_view: userfriendly and safe function for the creation of\n+        sliding window views.\n \n     Notes\n     -----\n@@ -111,6 +115,228 @@ def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n     return view\n \n \n+def _sliding_window_view_dispatcher(x, window_shape, axis=None, *,\n+                                    subok=None, writeable=None):\n+    return (x,)\n+\n+\n+@array_function_dispatch(_sliding_window_view_dispatcher, module='numpy')\n+def sliding_window_view(x, window_shape, axis=None, *,\n+                        subok=False, writeable=False):\n+    \"\"\"\n+    Create a sliding window view into the array with the given window shape.\n+\n+    Also known as rolling or moving window, the window slides across all\n+    dimensions of the array and extracts subsets of the array at all window\n+    positions.\n+    \n+    .. versionadded:: 1.20.0\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    x : array_like\n+        Array to create the sliding window view from.\n+    window_shape : int or tuple of int\n+        Size of window over each axis that takes part in the sliding window.\n+        If `axis` is not present, must have same length as the number of input\n+        array dimensions. Single integers `i` are treated as if they were the\n+        tuple `(i,)`.\n+    axis : int or tuple of int, optional\n+        Axis or axes along which the sliding window is applied.\n+        By default, the sliding window is applied to all axes and\n+        `window_shape[i]` will refer to axis `i` of `x`.\n+        If `axis` is given as a `tuple of int`, `window_shape[i]` will refer to\n+        the axis `axis[i]` of `x`.\n+        Single integers `i` are treated as if they were the tuple `(i,)`.\n+    subok : bool, optional\n+        If True, sub-classes will be passed-through, otherwise the returned\n+        array will be forced to be a base-class array (default).\n+    writeable : bool, optional\n+        When true, allow writing to the returned view. The default is false,\n+        as this should be used with caution: the returned view contains the\n+        same memory location multiple times, so writing to one location will\n+        cause others to change.\n+\n+    Returns\n+    -------\n+    view : ndarray\n+        Sliding window view of the array. The sliding window dimensions are\n+        inserted at the end, and the original dimensions are trimmed as\n+        required by the size of the sliding window.\n+        That is, ``view.shape = x_shape_trimmed + window_shape``, where\n+        ``x_shape_trimmed`` is ``x.shape`` with every entry reduced by one less\n+        than the corresponding window size.\n+\n+    See Also\n+    --------\n+    lib.stride_tricks.as_strided: A lower-level and less safe routine for\n+        creating arbitrary views from custom shape and strides.\n+    broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n+\n+    Notes\n+    -----\n+    For many applications using a sliding window view can be convenient, but\n+    potentially very slow. Often specialized solutions exist, for example:\n+\n+    - `scipy.signal.fftconvolve`\n+\n+    - filtering functions in `scipy.ndimage`\n+\n+    - moving window functions provided by\n+      `bottleneck <https://github.com/pydata/bottleneck>`_.\n+\n+    As a rough estimate, a sliding window approach with an input size of `N`\n+    and a window size of `W` will scale as `O(N*W)` where frequently a special\n+    algorithm can achieve `O(N)`. That means that the sliding window variant\n+    for a window size of 100 can be a 100 times slower than a more specialized\n+    version.\n+\n+    Nevertheless, for small window sizes, when no custom algorithm exists, or\n+    as a prototyping and developing tool, this function can be a good solution.\n+\n+    Examples\n+    --------\n+    >>> x = np.arange(6)\n+    >>> x.shape\n+    (6,)\n+    >>> v = np.sliding_window_view(x, 3)\n+    >>> v.shape\n+    (4, 3)\n+    >>> v\n+    array([[0, 1, 2],\n+           [1, 2, 3],\n+           [2, 3, 4],\n+           [3, 4, 5]])\n+\n+    This also works in more dimensions, e.g.\n+\n+    >>> i, j = np.ogrid[:3, :4]\n+    >>> x = 10*i + j\n+    >>> x.shape\n+    (3, 4)\n+    >>> x\n+    array([[ 0,  1,  2,  3],\n+           [10, 11, 12, 13],\n+           [20, 21, 22, 23]])\n+    >>> shape = (2,2)\n+    >>> v = np.sliding_window_view(x, shape)\n+    >>> v.shape\n+    (2, 3, 2, 2)\n+    >>> v\n+    array([[[[ 0,  1],\n+             [10, 11]],\n+            [[ 1,  2],\n+             [11, 12]],\n+            [[ 2,  3],\n+             [12, 13]]],\n+           [[[10, 11],\n+             [20, 21]],\n+            [[11, 12],\n+             [21, 22]],\n+            [[12, 13],\n+             [22, 23]]]])\n+\n+    The axis can be specified explicitly:\n+\n+    >>> v = np.sliding_window_view(x, 3, 0)\n+    >>> v.shape\n+    (1, 4, 3)\n+    >>> v\n+    array([[[ 0, 10, 20],\n+            [ 1, 11, 21],\n+            [ 2, 12, 22],\n+            [ 3, 13, 23]]])\n+\n+    The same axis can be used several times. In that case, every use reduces\n+    the corresponding original dimension:\n+\n+    >>> v = np.sliding_window_view(x, (2, 3), (1, 1))\n+    >>> v.shape\n+    (3, 1, 2, 3)\n+    >>> v\n+    array([[[[ 0,  1,  2],\n+             [ 1,  2,  3]]],\n+           [[[10, 11, 12],\n+             [11, 12, 13]]],\n+           [[[20, 21, 22],\n+             [21, 22, 23]]]])\n+\n+    Combining with stepped slicing (`::step`), this can be used to take sliding\n+    views which skip elements:\n+\n+    >>> x = np.arange(7)\n+    >>> np.sliding_window_view(x, 5)[:, ::2]\n+    array([[0, 2, 4],\n+           [1, 3, 5],\n+           [2, 4, 6]])\n+\n+    or views which move by multiple elements\n+\n+    >>> x = np.arange(7)\n+    >>> np.sliding_window_view(x, 3)[::2, :]\n+    array([[0, 1, 2],\n+           [2, 3, 4],\n+           [4, 5, 6]])\n+\n+    A common application of `sliding_window_view` is the calculation of running\n+    statistics. The simplest example is the\n+    `moving average <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moving_average>`_:\n+\n+    >>> x = np.arange(6)\n+    >>> x.shape\n+    (6,)\n+    >>> v = np.sliding_window_view(x, 3)\n+    >>> v.shape\n+    (4, 3)\n+    >>> v\n+    array([[0, 1, 2],\n+           [1, 2, 3],\n+           [2, 3, 4],\n+           [3, 4, 5]])\n+    >>> moving_average = v.mean(axis=-1)\n+    >>> moving_average\n+    array([1., 2., 3., 4.])\n+\n+    Note that a sliding window approach is often **not** optimal (see Notes).\n+    \"\"\"\n+    window_shape = (tuple(window_shape)\n+                    if np.iterable(window_shape)\n+                    else (window_shape,))\n+    # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n+    x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n+\n+    window_shape_array = np.array(window_shape)\n+    if np.any(window_shape_array < 0):\n+        raise ValueError('`window_shape` cannot contain negative values')\n+\n+    if axis is None:\n+        axis = tuple(range(x.ndim))\n+        if len(window_shape) != len(axis):\n+            raise ValueError(f'Since axis is `None`, must provide '\n+                             f'window_shape for all dimensions of `x`; '\n+                             f'got {len(window_shape)} window_shape elements '\n+                             f'and `x.ndim` is {x.ndim}.')\n+    else:\n+        axis = normalize_axis_tuple(axis, x.ndim, allow_duplicate=True)\n+        if len(window_shape) != len(axis):\n+            raise ValueError(f'Must provide matching length window_shape and '\n+                             f'axis; got {len(window_shape)} window_shape '\n+                             f'elements and {len(axis)} axes elements.')\n+\n+    out_strides = x.strides + tuple(x.strides[ax] for ax in axis)\n+\n+    # note: same axis can be windowed repeatedly\n+    x_shape_trimmed = list(x.shape)\n+    for ax, dim in zip(axis, window_shape):\n+        if x_shape_trimmed[ax] < dim:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                'window shape cannot be larger than input array shape')\n+        x_shape_trimmed[ax] -= dim - 1\n+    out_shape = tuple(x_shape_trimmed) + window_shape\n+    return as_strided(x, strides=out_strides, shape=out_shape,\n+                      subok=subok, writeable=writeable)\n+\n+\n def _broadcast_to(array, shape, subok, readonly):\n     shape = tuple(shape) if np.iterable(shape) else (shape,)\n     array = np.array(array, copy=False, subok=subok)\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 92764, "completion_tokens": 693}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'numpy' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - Use the unique artifacts provided to you to complement your understanding of the execution flow, or use as a hint.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: Suggestion: Sliding Window Function\n\nUsing `np.lib.stride_tricks.as_stride` one can very efficiently create a sliding window that segments an array as a preprocessing step for vectorized applications. For example a moving average of a window length `3`, stepsize `1`:\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = numpy.lib.stride_tricks.as_strided(\n    a, shape=(8, 3), strides=(8, 8)\n)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\nThis is very performant but very hard to do, as the `shape` and `strides` parameters are very hard to understand.\n\nI suggest the implementation of a \"simple `sliding_window` function\" that does the job of figuring out those two parameters for you.\n\n[The implementation I have been using for years](https://gist.github.com/nils-werner/9d321441006b112a4b116a8387c2280c) allows the above to be replaced by\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = sliding_window(a, size=3, stepsize=1)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\n@teoliphant [also has an implementation](https://gist.github.com/teoliphant/96eb779a16bd038e374f2703da62f06d) that would change the above to\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\na_strided = array_for_sliding_window(a, 3)\nprint numpy.mean(a_strided, axis=1)\n```\n\nboth making it much more readable.\n\nSeeing it is a common usecase in vectorized computing I suggest we put a similar function into NumPy itself.\n\nRegarding to which implementation to follow, they are both assume different things but allow you to do the same thing eventually:\n\n`sliding_window`\n- slides over one axis only\n- allows setting windowsize and stepsize\n- returns array with dimension n+1\n- sliding over several axes requires two calls (which come for free as there is no memory reordered)\n- has a superfluous `copy` parameter that can be removed and replaced by appending `.copy()` after the call\n\n`array_for_sliding_window`\n- slides over all axes simultaneously, window lengths are given as `tuple` parameter\n- Assumes a stepsize one in all directions\n- returns array with dimension n*2\n- stepsize not equal to one requires slicing of output data (unsure if this implies copying data)\n- Disabling sliding over `axis[n]` requires you set argument `wshape[n] = 1` or `wshape[n] = a.shape[n]`\n\nThis means for flexible `stepsize` the following are equivalent (with some minor bug in `sliding_window` there):\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(10)\nprint sliding_window(a, size=3, stepsize=2)\n\na = numpy.arange(10)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, 3)[::2, :] # Stepsize 2 by dropping every 2nd row\n```\n\nfor sliding over one `axis` the following are equivalent (with some transposing and squeezing):\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint sliding_window(a, size=3, axis=1)\n\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, (1, 3))\n```\n\nand for sliding over two `axis` the following are equivalent:\n\n```\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint sliding_window(sliding_window(a, size=3, axis=0), size=2, axis=1)\n\na = numpy.arange(25).reshape(5, 5)\nprint array_for_sliding_window(a, (3, 2))\n```\n\nThis issue is about sparking discussion about\n- Do we need such a function?\n- Which features are required?\n- Which interface should we persue?\n\nAfter discussion I am willing to draft up a pull request with an implementation we agreed on.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "I'll help analyze this feature request for a sliding window function implementation. Let me follow the steps systematically.\n\nStep 1: First, let me explore the repository structure to understand where relevant code might be located.\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['/']</parameter>\n<parameter=traversal_depth>2</parameter>\n<parameter=dependency_type_filter>['contains']</parameter>\n<parameter=entity_type_filter>['file', 'directory']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "explore_tree_structure", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 pavement.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 setup.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 runtests.py\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/openblas_support.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/download-wheels.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/refguide_check.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/cythonize.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/changelog.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/travis-sorter.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/commitstats.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/find_deprecated_escaped_characters.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/functions_missing_types.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/swig\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/ci\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/npy_tempita\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/c_coverage\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 tools/allocation_tracking\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/conftest.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/_globals.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/__init__.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/dual.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/_distributor_init.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/setup.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/ctypeslib.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/matlib.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/_pytesttester.py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/distutils\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/compat\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/core\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/linalg\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/ma\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/typing\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/f2py\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/lib\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/fft\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/doc\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/random\n\u2502               \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/matrixlib\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 numpy/polynomial\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 benchmarks\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 benchmarks/benchmarks\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/postprocess.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/example.py\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/cdoc\n                \u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/source\n                \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 doc/neps\n"}, {"content": "Let me search for the requested functionality in the provided implementation links. I'll first check for stride_tricks since it's directly related to the requested feature.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['stride_tricks', 'as_strided']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"stride_tricks\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `stride_tricks`.\nJust show the structure of this file due to response length limitations:\n```\n\n__all__ = ['broadcast_to', 'broadcast_arrays', 'broadcast_shapes']\n\n\nclass DummyArray:\n\n    def __init__(self, interface, base=None):\n...\n\ndef _maybe_view_as_subclass(original_array, new_array):\n...\n\ndef as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n...\n\ndef _broadcast_to(array, shape, subok, readonly):\n...\n\ndef _broadcast_to_dispatcher(array, shape, subok=None):\n...\n\n@array_function_dispatch(_broadcast_to_dispatcher, module='numpy')\ndef broadcast_to(array, shape, subok=False):\n...\n\ndef _broadcast_shape(*args):\n...\n\n@set_module('numpy')\ndef broadcast_shapes(*args):\n...\n\ndef _broadcast_arrays_dispatcher(*args, subok=None):\n...\n\n@array_function_dispatch(_broadcast_arrays_dispatcher, module='numpy')\ndef broadcast_arrays(*args, subok=False):\n...\n\n```\nHint: Search `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py` to get the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"as_strided\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `as_strided`.\n```\n 37 | def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n 38 |     \"\"\"\n 39 |     Create a view into the array with the given shape and strides.\n 40 | \n 41 |     .. warning:: This function has to be used with extreme care, see notes.\n 42 | \n 43 |     Parameters\n 44 |     ----------\n 45 |     x : ndarray\n 46 |         Array to create a new.\n 47 |     shape : sequence of int, optional\n 48 |         The shape of the new array. Defaults to ``x.shape``.\n 49 |     strides : sequence of int, optional\n 50 |         The strides of the new array. Defaults to ``x.strides``.\n 51 |     subok : bool, optional\n 52 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10\n 53 | \n 54 |         If True, subclasses are preserved.\n 55 |     writeable : bool, optional\n 56 |         .. versionadded:: 1.12\n 57 | \n 58 |         If set to False, the returned array will always be readonly.\n 59 |         Otherwise it will be writable if the original array was. It\n 60 |         is advisable to set this to False if possible (see Notes).\n 61 | \n 62 |     Returns\n 63 |     -------\n 64 |     view : ndarray\n 65 | \n 66 |     See also\n 67 |     --------\n 68 |     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n 69 |     reshape : reshape an array.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Notes\n 72 |     -----\n 73 |     ``as_strided`` creates a view into the array given the exact strides\n 74 |     and shape. This means it manipulates the internal data structure of\n 75 |     ndarray and, if done incorrectly, the array elements can point to\n 76 |     invalid memory and can corrupt results or crash your program.\n 77 |     It is advisable to always use the original ``x.strides`` when\n 78 |     calculating new strides to avoid reliance on a contiguous memory\n 79 |     layout.\n 80 | \n 81 |     Furthermore, arrays created with this function often contain self\n 82 |     overlapping memory, so that two elements are identical.\n 83 |     Vectorized write operations on such arrays will typically be\n 84 |     unpredictable. They may even give different results for small, large,\n 85 |     or transposed arrays.\n 86 |     Since writing to these arrays has to be tested and done with great\n 87 |     care, you may want to use ``writeable=False`` to avoid accidental write\n 88 |     operations.\n 89 | \n 90 |     For these reasons it is advisable to avoid ``as_strided`` when\n 91 |     possible.\n 92 |     \"\"\"\n 93 |     # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n 94 |     x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n 95 |     interface = dict(x.__array_interface__)\n 96 |     if shape is not None:\n 97 |         interface['shape'] = tuple(shape)\n 98 |     if strides is not None:\n 99 |         interface['strides'] = tuple(strides)\n100 | \n101 |     array = np.asarray(DummyArray(interface, base=x))\n102 |     # The route via `__interface__` does not preserve structured\n103 |     # dtypes. Since dtype should remain unchanged, we set it explicitly.\n104 |     array.dtype = x.dtype\n105 | \n106 |     view = _maybe_view_as_subclass(x, array)\n107 | \n108 |     if view.flags.writeable and not writeable:\n109 |         view.flags.writeable = False\n110 | \n111 |     return view\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the implementation mentioned by @teoliphant:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['array_for_sliding_window']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\ntools/travis-sorter.py:impl:3\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_function_attribute\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_version_at_least\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_restrict\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_compiler_gcc\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:new_ccompiler_opt\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_function_attribute_with_intrinsics\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_variable_attribute\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt._generate_config\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_dispatch_names\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_baseline_names\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_baseline_flags\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.is_cached\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.__init__\n##Searching for term \"array_for_sliding_window\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `numpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_inline`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"This module implements additional tests ala autoconf which can be useful.\n  2 | \n  3 | \"\"\"\n  4 | import textwrap\n  5 | \n  6 | # We put them here since they could be easily reused outside numpy.distutils\n  7 | \n  8 | def check_inline(cmd):\n  9 |     \"\"\"Return the inline identifier (may be empty).\"\"\"\n 10 |     cmd._check_compiler()\n 11 |     body = textwrap.dedent(\"\"\"\n 12 |         #ifndef __cplusplus\n 13 |         static %(inline)s int static_func (void)\n 14 |         {\n 15 |             return 0;\n 16 |         }\n 17 |         %(inline)s int nostatic_func (void)\n 18 |         {\n 19 |             return 0;\n 20 |         }\n 21 |         #endif\"\"\")\n 22 | \n 23 |     for kw in ['inline', '__inline__', '__inline']:\n 24 |         st = cmd.try_compile(body % {'inline': kw}, None, None)\n 25 |         if st:\n 26 |             return kw\n 27 | \n 28 |     return ''\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/lib/format.py:write_array`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n623 | def write_array(fp, array, version=None, allow_pickle=True, pickle_kwargs=None):\n624 |     \"\"\"\n625 |     Write an array to an NPY file, including a header.\n626 | \n627 |     If the array is neither C-contiguous nor Fortran-contiguous AND the\n628 |     file_like object is not a real file object, this function will have to\n629 |     copy data in memory.\n630 | \n631 |     Parameters\n632 |     ----------\n633 |     fp : file_like object\n634 |         An open, writable file object, or similar object with a\n635 |         ``.write()`` method.\n636 |     array : ndarray\n637 |         The array to write to disk.\n638 |     version : (int, int) or None, optional\n639 |         The version number of the format. None means use the oldest\n640 |         supported version that is able to store the data.  Default: None\n641 |     allow_pickle : bool, optional\n642 |         Whether to allow writing pickled data. Default: True\n643 |     pickle_kwargs : dict, optional\n644 |         Additional keyword arguments to pass to pickle.dump, excluding\n645 |         'p