 the input can be a sparse matrix.\n 30 | \n 31 |     categorical : bool, default=False\n 32 |         Whether the input can be categorical.\n 33 | \n 34 |     string : bool, default=False\n 35 |         Whether the input can be an array-like of strings.\n 36 | \n 37 |     dict : bool, default=False\n 38 |         Whether the input can be a dictionary.\n 39 | \n 40 |     positive_only : bool, default=False\n 41 |         Whether the estimator requires positive X.\n 42 | \n 43 |     allow_nan : bool, default=False\n 44 |         Whether the estimator supports data with missing values encoded as `np.nan`.\n 45 | \n 46 |     pairwise : bool, default=False\n 47 |         This boolean attribute indicates whether the data (`X`),\n 48 |         :term:`fit` and similar methods consists of pairwise measures\n 49 |         over samples rather than a feature representation for each\n 50 |         sample.  It is usually `True` where an estimator has a\n 51 |         `metric` or `affinity` or `kernel` parameter with value\n 52 |         'precomputed'. Its primary purpose is to support a\n 53 |         :term:`meta-estimator` or a cross validation procedure that\n 54 |         extracts a sub-sample of data intended for a pairwise\n 55 |         estimator, where the data needs to be indexed on both axes.\n 56 |         Specifically, this tag is used by\n 57 |         `sklearn.utils.metaestimators._safe_split` to slice rows and\n 58 |         columns.\n 59 |     \"\"\"\n 60 | \n 61 |     one_d_array: bool = False\n 62 |     two_d_array: bool = True\n 63 |     three_d_array: bool = False\n 64 |     sparse: bool = False\n 65 |     categorical: bool = False\n 66 |     string: bool = False\n 67 |     dict: bool = False\n 68 |     positive_only: bool = False\n 69 |     allow_nan: bool = False\n 70 |     pairwise: bool = False\n 71 | \n 72 | \n 73 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n 74 | class TargetTags:\n 75 |     \"\"\"Tags for the target data.\n 76 | \n 77 |     Parameters\n 78 |     ----------\n 79 |     required : bool\n 80 |         Whether the estimator requires y to be passed to `fit`,\n 81 |         `fit_predict` or `fit_transform` methods. The tag is ``True``\n 82 |         for estimators inheriting from `~sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`\n 83 |         and `~sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`.\n 84 | \n 85 |     one_d_labels : bool, default=False\n 86 |         Whether the input is a 1D labels (y).\n 87 | \n 88 |     two_d_labels : bool, default=False\n 89 |         Whether the input is a 2D labels (y).\n 90 | \n 91 |     positive_only : bool, default=False\n 92 |         Whether the estimator requires a positive y (only applicable\n 93 |         for regression).\n 94 | \n 95 |     multi_output : bool, default=False\n 96 |         Whether a regressor supports multi-target outputs or a classifier supports\n 97 |         multi-class multi-output.\n 98 | \n 99 |     single_output : bool, default=True\n100 |         Whether the target can be single-output. This can be ``False`` if the\n101 |         estimator supports only multi-output cases.\n102 |     \"\"\"\n103 | \n104 |     required: bool\n105 |     one_d_labels: bool = False\n106 |     two_d_labels: bool = False\n107 |     positive_only: bool = False\n108 |     multi_output: bool = False\n109 |     single_output: bool = True\n110 | \n111 | \n112 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n113 | class TransformerTags:\n114 |     \"\"\"Tags for the transformer.\n115 | \n116 |     Parameters\n117 |     ----------\n118 |     preserves_dtype : list[str], default=[\"float64\"]\n119 |         Applies only on transformers. It corresponds to the data types\n120 |         which will be preserved such that `X_trans.dtype` is the same\n121 |         as `X.dtype` after calling `transformer.transform(X)`. If this\n122 |         list is empty, then the transformer is not expected to\n123 |         preserve the data type. The first value in the list is\n124 |         considered as the default data type, corresponding to the data\n125 |         type of the output when the input data type is not going to be\n126 |         preserved.\n127 |     \"\"\"\n128 | \n129 |     preserves_dtype: list[str] = field(default_factory=lambda: [\"float64\"])\n130 | \n131 | \n132 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n133 | class ClassifierTags:\n134 |     \"\"\"Tags for the classifier.\n135 | \n136 |     Parameters\n137 |     ----------\n138 |     poor_score : bool, default=False\n139 |         Whether the estimator fails to provide a \"reasonable\" test-set\n140 |         score, which currently for classification is an accuracy of\n141 |         0.83 on ``make_blobs(n_samples=300, random_state=0)``. The\n142 |         datasets and values are based on current estimators in scikit-learn\n143 |         and might be replaced by something more systematic.\n144 | \n145 |     multi_class : bool, default=True\n146 |         Whether the classifier can handle multi-class\n147 |         classification. Note that all classifiers support binary\n148 |         classification. Therefore this flag indicates whether the\n149 |         classifier is a binary-classifier-only or not.\n150 | \n151 |     multi_label : bool, default=False\n152 |         Whether the classifier supports multi-label output.\n153 |     \"\"\"\n154 | \n155 |     poor_score: bool = False\n156 |     multi_class: bool = True\n157 |     multi_label: bool = False\n158 | \n159 | \n160 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n161 | class RegressorTags:\n162 |     \"\"\"Tags for the regressor.\n163 | \n164 |     Parameters\n165 |     ----------\n166 |     poor_score : bool, default=False\n167 |         Whether the estimator fails to provide a \"reasonable\" test-set\n168 |         score, which currently for regression is an R2 of 0.5 on\n169 |         ``make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=10,\n170 |         n_informative=1, bias=5.0, noise=20, random_state=42)``. The\n171 |         dataset and values are based on current estimators in scikit-learn\n172 |         and might be replaced by something more systematic.\n173 | \n174 |     multi_label : bool, default=False\n175 |         Whether the regressor supports multilabel output.\n176 |     \"\"\"\n177 | \n178 |     poor_score: bool = False\n179 |     multi_label: bool = False\n180 | \n181 | \n182 | @dataclass(**_dataclass_args())\n183 | class Tags:\n184 |     \"\"\"Tags for the estimator.\n185 | \n186 |     See :ref:`estimator_tags` for more information.\n187 | \n188 |     Parameters\n189 |     ----------\n190 |     estimator_type : str or None\n191 |         The type of the estimator. Can be one of:\n192 |         - \"classifier\"\n193 |         - \"regressor\"\n194 |         - \"transformer\"\n195 |         - \"clusterer\"\n196 |         - \"outlier_detector\"\n197 |         - \"density_estimator\"\n198 | \n199 |     target_tags : :class:`TargetTags`\n200 |         The target(y) tags.\n201 | \n202 |     transformer_tags : :class:`TransformerTags` or None\n203 |         The transformer tags.\n204 | \n205 |     classifier_tags : :class:`ClassifierTags` or None\n206 |         The classifier tags.\n207 | \n208 |     regressor_tags : :class:`RegressorTags` or None\n209 |         The regressor tags.\n210 | \n211 |     array_api_support : bool, default=False\n212 |         Whether the estimator supports Array API compatible inputs.\n213 | \n214 |     no_validation : bool, default=False\n215 |         Whether the estimator skips input-validation. This is only meant for\n216 |         stateless and dummy transformers!\n217 | \n218 |     non_deterministic : bool, default=False\n219 |         Whether the estimator is not deterministic given a fixed ``random_state``.\n220 | \n221 |     requires_fit : bool, default=True\n222 |         Whether the estimator requires to be fitted before calling one of\n223 |         `transform`, `predict`, `predict_proba`, or `decision_function`.\n224 | \n225 |     _skip_test : bool, default=False\n226 |         Whether to skip common tests entirely. Don't use this unless\n227 |         you have a *very good* reason.\n228 | \n229 |     input_tags : :class:`InputTags`\n230 |         The input data(X) tags.\n231 |     \"\"\"\n232 | \n233 |     estimator_type: str | None\n234 |     target_tags: TargetTags\n235 |     transformer_tags: TransformerTags | None = None\n236 |     classifier_tags: ClassifierTags | None = None\n237 |     regressor_tags: RegressorTags | None = None\n238 |     array_api_support: bool = False\n239 |     no_validation: bool = False\n240 |     non_deterministic: bool = False\n241 |     requires_fit: bool = True\n242 |     _skip_test: bool = False\n243 |     input_tags: InputTags = field(default_factory=InputTags)\n244 | \n245 | \n246 | # TODO(1.8): Remove this function\n247 | def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n248 |     \"\"\"Get the default tags for an estimator.\n249 | \n250 |     This ignores any ``__sklearn_tags__`` method that the estimator may have.\n251 | \n252 |     If the estimator is a classifier or a regressor, ``target_tags.required``\n253 |     will be set to ``True``, otherwise it will be set to ``False``.\n254 | \n255 |     ``transformer_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils. TransformerTags` if the\n256 |     estimator has a ``transform`` or ``fit_transform`` method, otherwise it will be set\n257 |     to ``None``.\n258 | \n259 |     ``classifier_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils.ClassifierTags` if the\n260 |     estimator is a classifier, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n261 |     a classifier, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n262 | \n263 |     ``regressor_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils.RegressorTags` if the\n264 |     estimator is a regressor, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n265 | \n266 |     Parameters\n267 |     ----------\n268 |     estimator : estimator object\n269 |         The estimator for which to get the default tags.\n270 | \n271 |     Returns\n272 |     -------\n273 |     tags : Tags\n274 |         The default tags for the estimator.\n275 |     \"\"\"\n276 |     est_is_classifier = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None) == \"classifier\"\n277 |     est_is_regressor = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None) == \"regressor\"\n278 |     target_required = est_is_classifier or est_is_regressor\n279 | \n280 |     return Tags(\n281 |         estimator_type=getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None),\n282 |         target_tags=TargetTags(required=target_required),\n283 |         transformer_tags=(\n284 |             TransformerTags()\n285 |             if hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n286 |             else None\n287 |         ),\n288 |         classifier_tags=ClassifierTags() if est_is_classifier else None,\n289 |         regressor_tags=RegressorTags() if est_is_regressor else None,\n290 |     )\n291 | \n292 | \n293 | def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n294 |     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n295 | \n296 |     :class:`~sklearn.BaseEstimator` provides the estimator tags machinery.\n297 |     However, if an estimator does not inherit from this base class, we should\n298 |     fall-back to the default tags.\n299 | \n300 |     For scikit-learn built-in estimators, we should still rely on\n301 |     `self.__sklearn_tags__()`. `get_tags(est)` should be used when we\n302 |     are not sure where `est` comes from: typically\n303 |     `get_tags(self.estimator)` where `self` is a meta-estimator, or in\n304 |     the common checks.\n305 | \n306 |     .. versionadded:: 1.6\n307 | \n308 |     Parameters\n309 |     ----------\n310 |     estimator : estimator object\n311 |         The estimator from which to get the tag.\n312 | \n313 |     Returns\n314 |     -------\n315 |     tags : :class:`~.sklearn.utils.Tags`\n316 |         The estimator tags.\n317 |     \"\"\"\n318 | \n319 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n320 |         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n321 |     else:\n322 |         warnings.warn(\n323 |             f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n324 |             \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n325 |             \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n326 |             \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n327 |             \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n328 |             \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n329 |             \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n330 |             category=FutureWarning,\n331 |         )\n332 |         tags = default_tags(estimator)\n333 | \n334 |     return tags\n335 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 319-332\nfunction: get_tags\n# Main location where old/new tag conversion logic needs to be implemented\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 246-250\nfunction: default_tags\n# Support function that gets called when __sklearn_tags__ is not present\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 392-399\nclass: BaseEstimator\nfunction: __sklearn_tags__\n# Base implementation that needs to be updated\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 4447-4466\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\n# Validation function that needs to be updated to handle tag conversion\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 2909-2910\nfunction: validate_data\n# Uses get_tags and needs to handle old/new tag compatibility\n```\n\nThese locations are critical for implementing the requested feature because:\n\n1. `get_tags` is the main function that needs to be modified to handle the conversion between old and new tag systems.\n\n2. `default_tags` is a supporting function that currently handles the fallback mechanism when `__sklearn_tags__` is not present.\n\n3. The `BaseEstimator` class is the foundation that defines the tag system, and its `__sklearn_tags__` method needs to be documented and enhanced.\n\n4. `check_estimator_tags_renamed` is responsible for validating the tag system and needs to be updated to handle the transition period.\n\n5. `validate_data` uses `get_tags` and needs to be modified to properly handle both old and new tag systems while maintaining compatibility.\n\nThe core functionality would need to:\n1. Detect whether an estimator is using the new or old system\n2. Provide appropriate warnings when transitioning between systems\n3. Maintain backward compatibility while updating the codebase\n4. Raise deprecation warnings for outdated practices\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 72379, "completion_tokens": 624}, "time": 88.74747395515442}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/accessors.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py", "pandas/core/base.py", "pandas/core/accessor.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/accessors.py:ArrowAccessor", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:NSort", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype", "pandas/_typing.py:SequenceNotStr", "asv_bench/benchmarks/strings.py:Methods", "pandas/io/parquet.py:BaseImpl", "asv_bench/benchmarks/rolling.py:EWMMethods", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/boxplot.py:BoxPlot", "scripts/validate_docstrings.py:PandasDocstring", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray", "asv_bench/benchmarks/io/json.py:ToJSONISO", "pandas/tseries/frequencies.py:_FrequencyInferer", "pandas/core/arrays/string_.py:StringDtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:AsOf", "pandas/core/computation/ops.py:Op", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray", "pandas/core/indexes/datetimelike.py:DatetimeIndexOpsMixin", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/accessors.py:ArrowAccessor._validate", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:NSort.time_nsmallest_one_column", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype.str", "pandas/_typing.py:SequenceNotStr.__getitem__", "pandas/_typing.py:SequenceNotStr.__iter__", "pandas/_typing.py:SequenceNotStr.index", "asv_bench/benchmarks/strings.py:Methods.time_isdecimal", "pandas/io/parquet.py:BaseImpl.write", "asv_bench/benchmarks/rolling.py:EWMMethods.time_ewm", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/boxplot.py:BoxPlot._caps_c", "scripts/validate_docstrings.py:PandasDocstring.name", "pandas/core/arrays/masked.py:BaseMaskedArray.dtype", "asv_bench/benchmarks/io/json.py:ToJSONISO.time_iso_format", "pandas/tseries/frequencies.py:_FrequencyInferer.is_unique_asi8", "pandas/core/arrays/string_.py:StringDtype.type", "asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:AsOf.time_asof_nan", "pandas/core/computation/ops.py:Op.__iter__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/timeseries.py:AsOf.time_asof_single", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray.isna", "pandas/core/indexes/datetimelike.py:DatetimeIndexOpsMixin._formatter_func", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor.__get__"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the relevant locations for implementing the requested feature. The issue is about improving error logging by providing more context about the data type mismatch in the string accessor feature.\n\nHere are the relevant locations:\n\n```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 257-258\nfunction: StringMethods._validate\n\npandas/core/arrays/arrow/accessors.py\nline: 50-51\nclass: ArrowAccessor\nfunction: _validate\n\npandas/core/dtypes/common.py\nline: 771-772\nfunction: DatetimeTZDtype.str\n\npandas/core/base.py\nline: 137-168\nclass: NoNewAttributesMixin\nfunction: _freeze, __setattr__\n\npandas/core/accessor.py\nline: 200-228\nclass: Accessor\nfunction: __get__\n```\n\nThe main location where the feature should be implemented is in `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py` at line 257-258, where the current error message is defined. The code currently raises a generic error message without the data type information, which according to the issue description needs to be updated to include the inferred data type.\n\nThe other locations are included for context as they are related to the string accessor feature and its error handling mechanism:\n1. `ArrowAccessor._validate` in arrow accessors.py shows how attribute errors are handled in the broader context\n2. `DatetimeTZDtype.str` shows the pattern for formatting type information consistently\n3. `NoNewAttributesMixin` in base.py shows the pattern for proper error message formatting\n4. `Accessor.__get__` in accessor.py shows how the accessor mechanism works"], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 56046, "completion_tokens": 551}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified enti