u have a\n852 |         Series.\n853 | \n854 |         Parameters\n855 |         ----------\n856 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}, default None\n857 |             A specific axis to squeeze. By default, all length-1 axes are\n858 |             squeezed. For `Series` this parameter is unused and defaults to `None`.\n859 | \n860 |         Returns\n861 |         -------\n862 |         DataFrame, Series, or scalar\n863 |             The projection after squeezing `axis` or all the axes.\n864 | \n865 |         See Also\n866 |         --------\n867 |         Series.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting scalars.\n868 |         DataFrame.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting Series.\n869 |         Series.to_frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.rename_axis`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1103 | def rename_axis(\n1104 |         self,\n1105 |         mapper: IndexLabel | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n1106 |         *,\n1107 |         index=lib.no_default,\n1108 |         columns=lib.no_default,\n1109 |         axis: Axis = 0,\n1110 |         copy: bool | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n1111 |         inplace: bool = False,\n1112 |     ) -> Self | None:\n1113 |         \"\"\"\n1114 |         Set the name of the axis for the index or columns.\n1115 | \n1116 |         Parameters\n1117 |         ----------\n1118 |         mapper : scalar, list-like, optional\n1119 |             Value to set the axis name attribute.\n1120 | \n1121 |             Use either ``mapper`` and ``axis`` to\n1122 |             specify the axis to target with ``mapper``, or ``index``\n1123 |             and/or ``columns``.\n1124 |         index : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1125 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1126 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1127 |         columns : scalar, list-like, dict-like or function, optional\n1128 |             A scalar, list-like, dict-like or functions transformations to\n1129 |             apply to that axis' values.\n1130 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns'}, default 0\n1131 |             The axis to rename.\n1132 |         copy : bool, default False\n1133 |             Also copy underlying data.\n1134 | \n1135 |             .. note::\n1136 |                 The `copy` keyword will change behavior in pandas 3.0.\n1137 |                 `Copy-on-Write\n1138 |                 <https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/user_guide/copy_on_write.html>`__\n1139 |                 will be enabled by default, which means that all methods with a\n1140 |                 `copy` keyword will use a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n1141 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n1142 |                 future version of pandas.\n1143 | \n1144 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n1145 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n1146 | \n1147 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n1148 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1149 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Series\n1150 |             or DataFrame.\n1151 | \n1152 |         Returns\n1153 |         -------\n1154 |         DataFrame, or None\n1155 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1156 | \n1157 |         See Also\n1158 |         --------\n1159 |         Series.rename : Alter Series index labels or name.\n1160 |         DataFrame.rename : Alter DataFrame index labels or name.\n1161 |         Index.rename : Set new names on index.\n1162 | \n1163 |         Notes\n1164 |         -----\n1165 |         ``DataFrame.rename_axis`` supports two calling conventions\n1166 | \n1167 |         * ``(index=index_mapper, columns=columns_mapper, ...)``\n1168 |         * ``(mapper, axis={'index', 'columns'}, ...)``\n1169 | \n1170 |         The first calling convention will only modify the names of\n1171 |         the index and/or the names of the Index object that is the columns.\n1172 |         In this case, the parameter ``copy`` is ignored.\n1173 | \n1174 |         The second calling convention will modify the names of the\n1175 |         corresponding index if mapper is a list or a scalar.\n1176 |         However, if mapper is dict-like or a function, it will use the\n1177 |         deprecated behavior of modifying the axis *labels*.\n1178 | \n1179 |         We *highly* recommend using keyword arguments to clarify your\n1180 |         intent.\n1181 | \n1182 |         Examples\n1183 |         --------\n1184 |         **DataFrame**\n1185 | \n1186 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1187 |         ...     {\"num_legs\": [4, 4, 2], \"num_arms\": [0, 0, 2]}, [\"dog\", \"cat\", \"monkey\"]\n1188 |         ... )\n1189 |         >>> df\n1190 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1191 |         dog            4         0\n1192 |         cat            4         0\n1193 |         monkey         2         2\n1194 |         >>> df = df.rename_axis(\"animal\")\n1195 |         >>> df\n1196 |                 num_legs  num_arms\n1197 |         animal\n1198 |         dog            4         0\n1199 | 